  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Compute multiple statistical aggregations (mean, sum, min, max) across categorical groups in a pandas DataFrame.\n\nAggregating multiple reduction statistics simultaneously captures both central tendency and extreme dispersion limits.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\operatorname{Agg}_f(G) = f(\\mathbf{y}_{g}) \\quad \\forall f \\in \\{\\text{mean, sum, min, max}\\}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Group records by categorical group_col\n2. Apply mean, sum, min, and max aggregations to numerical value_col\n3. Structure outputs into a nested dictionary mapped by aggregation function name\n\n### Returns\nDictionary mapping each aggregation function to a dictionary of group labels and values.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Aggregation Functions\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Aggregation Functions** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nAggregating multiple reduction statistics simultaneously captures both central tendency and extreme dispersion limits. In this problem, the goal is to: compute multiple statistical aggregations (mean, sum, min, max) across categorical groups in a pandas dataframe.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Agg}_f(G) = f(\\mathbf{y}_{g}) \\quad \\forall f \\in \\{\\text{mean, sum, min, max}\\}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Group records by categorical group_col\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply mean, sum, min, and max aggregations to numerical value_col\n3. **Phase 3**: Structure outputs into a nested dictionary mapped by aggregation function name\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"grp\": \"A\", \"val\": 10}, {\"grp\": \"A\", \"val\": 20}, {\"grp\": \"B\", \"val\": 30}], group_col = \"grp\", value_col = \"val\"\n\nOutput: {\"mean\": {\"A\": 15.0, \"B\": 30.0}, \"sum\": {\"A\": 30.0, \"B\": 30.0}, \"min\": {\"A\": 10.0, \"B\": 30.0}, \"max\": {\"A\": 20.0, \"B\": 30.0}}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_aggregation_functions under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_aggregation_functions`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
